I'd like to talk about a time when I changed my opinion about a type of food.

It happened about four years ago when my friends and I took a trip to Phú Quốc Island. At that time, we were all quite overwhelmed with work and studies, so we decided to take a short vacation to relax and recharge. During the trip, we visited several tourist attractions and spent a lot of time exploring the local cuisine.

Before the trip, I was responsible for researching famous local dishes, and one of the specialties I came across was gỏi cá trích, or Vietnamese herring salad. When we visited a local restaurant, my friends suggested that we order it. To be honest, I was quite hesitant because I had never eaten raw fish before, and the dish looked a bit intimidating to me.

My friends kept encouraging me to give it a try, so eventually I decided to take a small bite. To my surprise, I found it not only edible but actually delicious. The dish featured fresh herring mixed with herbs, shredded coconut, onions, and a flavorful dipping sauce. What impressed me most was the balance of flavors. It was slightly sweet, a little sour, and incredibly refreshing at the same time.

Before that experience, I had always assumed that dishes made with raw fish would taste unpleasant or have a strong fishy smell. However, after trying the herring salad, my opinion changed completely. I realized that I had judged the dish based on my assumptions rather than actual experience.

As a result, I became much more open-minded about trying unfamiliar foods. During the rest of the trip, I eagerly explored many other local specialties that I would have avoided in the past.

Looking back, I felt both surprised and excited because the experience taught me an important lesson: it's always worth stepping outside your comfort zone and trying something new. Sometimes, the things we are most hesitant about turn out to be the things we enjoy the most.
